WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.900
At 23 years old, I was in my third season working as a glacier guide in Alaska.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.520
I took people hiking on glaciers, ice climbing on glaciers,

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:18.260
and that spring I had successfully summited the 16,000 foot glaciated volcano that looms over town.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:25.940
I was feeling very confident in my skills in the mountains and I could finally grow a full beard.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:32.320
So when my friend Elizabeth asked if I wanted to be her assistant on a fly-in base

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:39.240
camping trip, I said, oh yeah, that sounds super chill.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.680
She told me the four clients were the women that I had met that afternoon on the deck

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:52.140
of the guide service. They were decked out head to toe in brand-new Arc'teryx rain gear,

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.080
which is a red flag.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:02.920
They were from New York City and had never been camping.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.560
They had never slept in a tent.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.020
I lived in a tent.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:18.160
So all six of us get into an airplane the size of a minivan with wings.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.980
We get to the head of the valley and we see that the easiest way into the alpine

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.080
is over this glacier.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.000
And Elizabeth and I take people hiking on glaciers all summer long.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.480
We don't have our full kit but it's good weather,

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.860
good hikers, we're good to go.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.900
And when we get up on the ice everything changes.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:40.820
And then we encounter a little bit of snow and then a little bit more snow.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.252
And when you are on a winter glacier that's covered in snow it's customary to rope yourself to

00:01:45.252 --> 00:01:51.192
each other in case somebody unexpectedly breaks through the snow and falls into a crevasse.

00:01:51.192 --> 00:01:56.612
So Elizabeth goes ahead to scout our way off of the ice which can be problematic and I

00:01:56.612 --> 00:02:01.852
stay behind guiding our clients and I've got them behind me in a single file line and I'm

00:02:01.852 --> 00:02:05.792
making sure to stay as much as we can on the exposed ice,

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.792
and if we have to step on snow at all,

00:02:08.792 --> 00:02:12.992
I use my ski pole to probe it first to make sure that it's snow over ice,

00:02:12.992 --> 00:02:14.752
not snow over air.

00:02:14.752 --> 00:02:19.272
And now I'm having a good time.

00:02:20.732 --> 00:02:22.692
I'm moving my clients through terrain.

00:02:22.692 --> 00:02:25.352
I'm picking lines that are mostly ice.

00:02:25.352 --> 00:02:27.052
If I got to deal with a little bit of snow,

00:02:27.052 --> 00:02:28.872
I probe. I step.

00:02:28.872 --> 00:02:30.852
I probe. I step.

00:02:30.852 --> 00:02:34.732
I'm thinking, I am a good guide.

00:02:35.372 --> 00:02:40.292
Then the next step I take is just in front of my probe pole,

00:02:40.292 --> 00:02:43.512
and the snow beneath me disintegrates instantly,

00:02:43.512 --> 00:02:49.932
and I begin to free fall into the glacier immediately.

00:02:49.932 --> 00:02:57.332
And for long enough to think a few things.

00:02:58.652 --> 00:03:04.872
First, I'm going to die, followed by,

00:03:04.872 --> 00:03:08.092
what an embarrassing way for a glacier guide to die.

00:03:11.412 --> 00:03:13.812
And then, hey, let's try and not die.

00:03:14.272 --> 00:03:18.372
And I put my arms and legs and ski pole out to try and slow me down,

00:03:18.372 --> 00:03:22.672
and I come to a stop on this rotten pile of snow.

00:03:23.852 --> 00:03:28.244
I'm 20 feet below the surface of the glacier.

00:03:28.244 --> 00:03:32.184
It is dark and cold.

00:03:32.184 --> 00:03:39.144
It's like a long, narrow hallway with tall ceilings and a single skylight.

00:03:39.404 --> 00:03:44.044
I have been inside glaciers before but never involuntarily.

00:03:44.044 --> 00:03:51.044
And I just left the four New York never-campers who had just spent their first night in

00:03:51.044 --> 00:03:55.124
a tent after being flown into the middle of nowhere and then watched the guy they brought to

00:03:55.124 --> 00:04:00.144
protect them from the wilderness vanish into thin air.

00:04:00.144 --> 00:04:05.684
Finally Elizabeth looked down at me through the hole that I had punched in the snow.

00:04:05.684 --> 00:04:12.524
We made eye contact and neither of us needed to say we should have brought a rope.

00:04:15.604 --> 00:04:21.284
We went into problem-solving mode,

00:04:21.284 --> 00:04:24.944
and I told her I thought that I could climb up a ways,

00:04:24.944 --> 00:04:29.804
but I didn't know how to get past the overhanging lip of snow at the top of the crack.

00:04:29.804 --> 00:04:33.244
And Elizabeth's eyes lit up, and she said,

00:04:33.244 --> 00:04:36.704
if you can get up here, I can get you past that lip.

00:04:37.484 --> 00:04:39.104
And then she disappeared.

00:04:39.104 --> 00:04:43.884
By the time I started climbing, I had been in this crevasse for at least 20 minutes.

00:04:43.884 --> 00:04:46.964
And I climb up five feet and 10 feet.

00:04:46.964 --> 00:04:52.524
And then I look down and I do not like what I see.

00:04:52.704 --> 00:04:59.624
This snow I had landed on looks like cotton candy and there's darkness through the holes.

00:04:59.624 --> 00:05:03.344
And any time that I knock an ice crystal through them,

00:05:03.344 --> 00:05:06.344
I cannot hear it hit the bottom.

00:05:06.344 --> 00:05:11.916
I have just turned a relaxing weekend in the wilderness into a life or death situation,

00:05:11.916 --> 00:05:14.077
and I'm terrified.

00:05:16.296 --> 00:05:22.777
And just then, I hear the flap of fabric and a flash of color,

00:05:22.777 --> 00:05:32.856
and the first of four brightly colored brand-new Arc'teryx

00:05:32.856 --> 00:05:40.257
rain jackets tied sleeve to sleeve is

00:05:40.257 --> 00:05:45.637
lowered down in front of me and I had never been so happy.

00:05:45.637 --> 00:05:47.937
My clients had brand-new rain gear.

00:05:47.937 --> 00:06:00.096
And I grab a hold of what is at least a $3,000 rope.

00:06:00.096 --> 00:06:06.197
And Elizabeth and those New Yorkers pulled me past the lip and into the sunshine.

00:06:06.197 --> 00:06:09.216
And when I got there, I was embarrassed.

00:06:10.957 --> 00:06:12.697
But they tried to soothe me.

00:06:12.697 --> 00:06:15.877
They said things like, we're just really impressed that you climbed out of there,

00:06:15.877 --> 00:06:18.916
and you know everyone makes mistakes.

00:06:18.916 --> 00:06:21.476
I myself didn't tell anybody for years.

00:06:21.476 --> 00:06:23.377
I was so ashamed of this incident,

00:06:23.377 --> 00:06:28.517
but these days I own that guide service and I tell all the new guides this story under

00:06:28.517 --> 00:06:33.656
the heading, the glacier does not care how full your beard is.

00:06:37.377 --> 00:06:43.257
Because the truth is we all do make mistakes and the real shame is not being able to

00:06:43.257 --> 00:06:46.637
admit them and share them with others so that we can all learn.

00:06:46.637 --> 00:06:48.497
Thank you.
